      "content": {
        "raw_text": "\n--- Page 259 ---\n224\nSPHERICAL HARMONICS.\n[146.\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\Psi=h_{0}+h_{1} & Y_{1} r+\\text { \\&c. }+h_{n}^{(\\sigma)} Y_{n}^{(\\sigma)} r^{n}+\\ldots \\\\\n& +k_{0}^{1}+k_{1} Y_{1} \\frac{1}{r^{2}}+\\text { \\&c. }+k_{n}^{(\\sigma)} Y_{n}^{(\\sigma)} \\frac{1}{r^{n+1}}+\\cdots,\n\\end{aligned}$$\n(21)\nthen we have to determine the constants of the forms $h$ and $k$ so\nthat when $r=a(1+F), \\Psi=a$, and when $r=b(1+G), \\Psi=\\beta$.\nIt is manifest, from our former investigation, that all the $h$ 's\nand $k$ 's except $h_{0}$ and $k_{0}$ will be small quantities, the products of\nwhich into $F$ may be neglected. We may, therefore, write\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n& a=h_{0}+k_{0} \\frac{1}{a}(1-F)+\\text { \\&c. }+\\left(h_{n}^{(\\sigma)} u^{n}+k_{n}^{(\\sigma)} \\frac{1}{a^{n+1}}\\right) Y_{n}^{(\\sigma)}+\\cdots \\\\\n& \\beta=h_{0}+k_{0} \\frac{1}{b}(1-G)+\\text { \\&c. }+\\left(h_{n}^{(\\sigma)} b^{n}+k_{n}^{(\\sigma)} \\frac{1}{b^{n+1}}\\right) Y_{n}^{(\\sigma)}+\\cdots\n\\end{aligned}$$\n(22)\n(23)\nWe have therefore\n$$\\begin{aligned}\na & =h_{0}+k_{0} \\frac{1}{a} \\\\\n\\beta & =h_{0}+k_{0} \\frac{1}{b} \\\\\nk_{0} \\frac{1}{a} f_{n}^{(\\sigma)} & =h_{n}^{(\\sigma)} u^{n}+k_{n}^{(\\sigma)} \\frac{1}{a^{n+1}} \\\\\nk_{0} \\frac{1}{b} g_{n}^{(\\sigma)} & =h_{n}^{(\\sigma)} b^{n}+k_{n}^{(\\sigma)} \\frac{1}{b^{n+1}}\n\\end{aligned}$$\n(24)\n(25)\n(26)\n(27)\nwhence we find for $k_{0}$, the charge of the inner conductor,\n$$k_{0}=(\\alpha-\\beta) \\frac{a b}{b-a}$$\n(28)\nand for the coefficients of the harmonics of order $n$\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n& h_{n}^{(\\sigma)}=k_{0} \\frac{b^{n} g_{n}^{(\\sigma)}-a^{n} f_{n}^{(\\sigma)}}{b^{2 n+1}-a^{2 n+1}} \\\\\n& k_{n}^{(\\sigma)}=k_{0} a^{n} b^{n} \\frac{b^{n+1} f_{n}^{(\\sigma)}-a^{n+1} g_{n}^{(\\sigma)}}{b^{2 n+1}-a^{2 n+1}}\n\\end{aligned}$$\n(29)\n(30)\nwhere we must remember that the coefficients $f_{n}^{(\\sigma)}, g_{n}^{(\\tau)}, h_{n}^{(\\sigma)}, k_{n}^{(\\sigma)}$ are\nthose belonging to the same type as well as order.\nThe surface density on the inner conductor is given by the\nequation\n$$4 \\pi \\sigma a^{2}=k_{0}\\left(1+\\ldots+A_{n} Y_{n}^{(\\sigma)}+\\ldots\\right)$$\nwhere $A_{n}=\\frac{f_{n}^{(\\sigma)}\\left\\{(n+2) a^{2 n+1}+(n-1) b^{2 n+1}\\right\\}-g_{n}^{(\\sigma)}(2 n+1) a^{n+1} b^{n}}{b^{2 n+1}-a^{2 n+1}}$.\n(31)\n146.] As an example of the application of zonal harmonics,\nlet us investigate the equilibrium of electricity on two spherical\nconductors.\n",
        "mathpix_markdown": "\n--- Page 259 ---\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\Psi=h_{0}+h_{1} & Y_{1} r+\\text { \\&c. }+h_{n}^{(\\sigma)} Y_{n}^{(\\sigma)} r^{n}+\\ldots \\\\\n& +k_{0}^{1}+k_{1} Y_{1} \\frac{1}{r^{2}}+\\text { \\&c. }+k_{n}^{(\\sigma)} Y_{n}^{(\\sigma)} \\frac{1}{r^{n+1}}+\\cdots, \\tag{21}\n\\end{align*}$$\n\nthen we have to determine the constants of the forms $h$ and $k$ so\n that when $r=a(1+F), \\Psi=a$, and when $r=b(1+G), \\Psi=\\beta$.\n\n\nIt is manifest, from our former investigation, that all the $h$ 's\n and $k$ 's except $h_{0}$ and $k_{0}$ will be small quantities, the products of\n which into $F$ may be neglected. We may, therefore, write\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\n& a=h_{0}+k_{0} \\frac{1}{a}(1-F)+\\text { \\&c. }+\\left(h_{n}^{(\\sigma)} u^{n}+k_{n}^{(\\sigma)} \\frac{1}{a^{n+1}}\\right) Y_{n}^{(\\sigma)}+\\cdots  \\tag{22}\\\\\n& \\beta=h_{0}+k_{0} \\frac{1}{b}(1-G)+\\text { \\&c. }+\\left(h_{n}^{(\\sigma)} b^{n}+k_{n}^{(\\sigma)} \\frac{1}{b^{n+1}}\\right) Y_{n}^{(\\sigma)}+\\cdots \\tag{23}\n\\end{align*}$$\n\n\nWe have therefore\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\na & =h_{0}+k_{0} \\frac{1}{a}  \\tag{24}\\\\\n\\beta & =h_{0}+k_{0} \\frac{1}{b}  \\tag{25}\\\\\nk_{0} \\frac{1}{a} f_{n}^{(\\sigma)} & =h_{n}^{(\\sigma)} u^{n}+k_{n}^{(\\sigma)} \\frac{1}{a^{n+1}}  \\tag{26}\\\\\nk_{0} \\frac{1}{b} g_{n}^{(\\sigma)} & =h_{n}^{(\\sigma)} b^{n}+k_{n}^{(\\sigma)} \\frac{1}{b^{n+1}} \\tag{27}\n\\end{align*}$$\n\nwhence we find for $k_{0}$, the charge of the inner conductor,\n\n$$\\begin{equation*}\nk_{0}=(\\alpha-\\beta) \\frac{a b}{b-a} \\tag{28}\n\\end{equation*}$$\n\nand for the coefficients of the harmonics of order $n$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\n& h_{n}^{(\\sigma)}=k_{0} \\frac{b^{n} g_{n}^{(\\sigma)}-a^{n} f_{n}^{(\\sigma)}}{b^{2 n+1}-a^{2 n+1}}  \\tag{29}\\\\\n& k_{n}^{(\\sigma)}=k_{0} a^{n} b^{n} \\frac{b^{n+1} f_{n}^{(\\sigma)}-a^{n+1} g_{n}^{(\\sigma)}}{b^{2 n+1}-a^{2 n+1}} \\tag{30}\n\\end{align*}$$\n\nwhere we must remember that the coefficients $f_{n}^{(\\sigma)}, g_{n}^{(\\tau)}, h_{n}^{(\\sigma)}, k_{n}^{(\\sigma)}$ are\n those belonging to the same type as well as order.\n\n\nThe surface density on the inner conductor is given by the\n equation\n\n$$\\begin{equation*}\n4 \\pi \\sigma a^{2}=k_{0}\\left(1+\\ldots+A_{n} Y_{n}^{(\\sigma)}+\\ldots\\right) \\tag{31}\n\\end{equation*}$$\n\nwhere $A_{n}=\\frac{f_{n}^{(\\sigma)}\\left\\{(n+2) a^{2 n+1}+(n-1) b^{2 n+1}\\right\\}-g_{n}^{(\\sigma)}(2 n+1) a^{n+1} b^{n}}{b^{2 n+1}-a^{2 n+1}}$.\n\n146.] As an example of the application of zonal harmonics,\n let us investigate the equilibrium of electricity on two spherical\n conductors.\n",
